<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Philippe Villiers de L'Isle-Adam</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1464">1464</a>–<a href="/years/1534">1534</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Philippe Villiers de L'Isle-Adam was Grand Master of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-hospitaller">Knights Hospitaller</a> from 1521. He led the defence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suleiman-the-magnificent">Suleiman the Magnificent</a> in 1522 and, after its surrender, the Order's years of wandering. In 1530 he accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gozo">Gozo</a> and Tripoli from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> and settled the Order at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/birgu">Birgu</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philippe-villiers-de-lisle-adam.jpg" alt="Philippe Villiers de L'Isle-Adam" width="320" height="390" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Villiers_de_l_Isle-Adam.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Fra' Philippe de Villiers de L'Isle-Adam was a prominent member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-hospitaller">Knights Hospitaller</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> and later at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a>. He rose to be Prior of the Langue of Auvergne and in 1521 was elected the 44th Grand Master of the Order.</p><p>He commanded the Order during Sultan Suleiman's long and bloody Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> in 1522, when 600 knights and 4,500 soldiers resisted an invading force of about 100,000 men for six months. He eventually negotiated the capitulation, and the knights left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> on New Year's Day 1523. The Order then spent several years without a permanent home, at Kandi on Crete, then at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/messina">Messina</a>, Viterbo and finally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nice">Nice</a>, where it stayed from 1527 to 1529.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>In 1530 L'Isle-Adam obtained from Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> the islands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gozo">Gozo</a> and the North African port of Tripoli as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> for the Order, which from then on was known as the Maltese Knights. The Order reached the island on 26 October 1530 on its flagship, the Santa Anna, and L'Isle-Adam took formal possession on 13 November, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> key of Mdina was given to him. The Order nevertheless settled in the coastal town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/birgu">Birgu</a> and made it its capital, living in Fort Saint Angelo, which served as both fortress and palace. The Grand Master and the Order still hoped to recapture <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> one day.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>L'Isle-Adam died on 21 August 1534 at the convent of Our Lady of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabat">Rabat</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a>. He was buried in the Chapel of St Anne within Fort Saint Angelo, and was reburied in the crypt of Saint John's Co-Cathedral in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valletta">Valletta</a> in the late 16th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Philippe Villiers de L'Isle-Adam” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Grand Master <b>Philippe Villiers de L'Isle-Adam</b> settled the Order at Birgu.</blockquote><a href="/state/hospitaller-malta">Hospitaller Malta</a></li><li><blockquote>After six months Grand Master <b>Philippe Villiers de l'Isle-Adam</b> surrendered on honourable terms, and the knights left in early 1523, settling in Malta in 1530.</blockquote><a href="/state/hospitaller-rhodes">Hospitaller Rhodes</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beauvais">Beauvais</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Rabat. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.43,2.09&amp;year=1509" aria-label="Beauvais on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4598 441 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Philippe Villiers de L'Isle-Adam"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5048 711h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5048 711h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.43,2.09&amp;year=1509">What was here in 1509? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
